  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Thunderstorms this afternoon and evening east of US-93 through\n   10pm MDT bringing risk of lightning and strong outflow gusts.\n\n-  Critical fire weather this weekend.\n\n-  Cooldown for Sunday and Monday with temperatures rebounding to\n   near/above normal by Tuesday and Wednesday of next week.\n\nHot, dry, and smoky conditions remain entrenched today with high-\nbased, inverted-V profile, dry thunderstorms developing this \nafternoon primarily east of US-93 and along the HWY 200 corridor. \nRain totals will be minimal and isolated, primary hazards from\nconvection today between 4pm and 10 pm MDT will be lightning (and\nany new fire starts that occur) and strong erratic outflow winds \ngusting 30 to 45 mph (low chance for an isolated 50+ mph). Friday \nwill be mostly convection-free, hot, dry, and smoky serving as the\ncalm (and continuing to pre-condition wildland fuels) before this\nweekend's weather event.\n\nHigh confidence remains in a critical fire weather pattern this\nweekend as a dry Pacific trough pushes a cold front through the\nregion. Pre-frontal heating peaks on Saturday with temperatures \napproaching 100 degrees in valleys south of I-90 and in southwest \nMontana. Widespread westerly winds ramp up Saturday afternoon, \ngusting 25 to 40 mph (with ridgetops approaching 50 mph) alongside\nrelative humidities dropping to at least 10-15%. Current Fire \nWeather Watches remain active, with Red Flag decisions planned to \nbe finalized on Friday's day shift. Post- frontal cold air \nadvection drops temperatures 10 to 20 degrees for Sunday and \nMonday, though strong westerly breezes persist across the Northern\nRockies on Sunday keeping SW Montana in critical fire weather \nconditions through Sunday evening. \n\nNorthwest flow dominates Monday and Tuesday, keeping highs below\nseasonal averages with cool overnight lows. Isolated mountain\nshowers are possible but moisture will be severely limited. A \nthermal ridge begins to rebuild by midweek next week marking the \nreturn of above normal high temperatures.",
